export const body = "<p>Chapter 03: Visual Storytelling</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>In Chapter 1, we briefly mentioned “story” as one component of level creation. As an interactive medium, video games can present story elements within the environments our players explore.</p>\n\n<p>These three levels cascade from top to bottom to drive the storytelling in your game world. For level artists, the primary focus is the environment level, but we will briefly go through the entire hierarchy to understand the big picture.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0c15874d27d8c0b9c8b8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0c15874d27d8c0b9c8b8.webp\" width=\"1054\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Worldbuilding</h2>\n\n<p><strong>What is it?</strong></p>\n\n<p>World building is a common process in writing, particularly in the sci-fi and fantasy genres. The meaning is straightforward: it’s the act of building a fictional world.</p>\n\n<p>As a level artist, you should study the history of your game world so that you can fabricate environments that fit into the established visual framework.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/80e10129fd9a1d66d3cb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/80e10129fd9a1d66d3cb.webp\" width=\"685\" height=\"563\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Setting</h2>\n\n<p>What is it?</p>\n\n<p>A setting is where the story takes place within the world. For example, in the first <em>film in the ser</em>ies, we have the world as “a galaxy far far away,” but the settings are Tatooine, Yavin Moon, and Death Star.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9c59ad4613cec8d803d6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9c59ad4613cec8d803d6.webp\" width=\"714\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:427px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>World vs. Setting</h2>\n\n<p>An example</p>\n\n<p>In <em>The Lord of The Rings</em>, Middle-Earth is the world, and the individual locales (The Shire and Minas Tirith, for example) are settings. The world provides the story with high-level concepts such as the magic system, races, technology, etc.</p>\n\n<p>Meanwhile, the settings invite us into the different cultures present in the story: The Shire is surrounded by nature and has small, circular dwellings; Minas Tirith, on the other hand, resides on a high mountain and its architectural style is defined by rectangular shapes.</p>\n\n<p>In short, you can think of <em>world</em> as the universe of your game and each <em>setting</em> as a level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/516232dfde3d32e47320.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/516232dfde3d32e47320.webp\" width=\"823\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:492px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong> – <strong>World vs. Setting</strong></p>\n\n<p>In <em>Breath of the Wild</em>, our world is the kingdom of Hyrule, a land that fell from grace due to the influence of the main villain Ganon. To restore the unity and freedom of Hyrule, Link is set on a quest to liberate 4 Divine Beasts residing in 4 different settings in the world.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b0c82509ef40a83bf981.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b0c82509ef40a83bf981.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"328\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Environmental storytelling</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>Once you know your game world and the setting of your level, you can begin to work on the lowest level of visual storytelling: environment.</p>\n\n<p>Environmental storytelling is the act of formulating and staging a level with meaningful environmental properties. These properties include the locale of your level, set dressing props, textures, and even character models.</p>\n\n<p>By adding story elements, you can flesh out the world visually, encouraging the player to pay greater attention to their surroundings. This can all translate to greater engagement and higher emotional investment as the player continuously discovers what happened.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d47b07dfa77afb6dc381.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d47b07dfa77afb6dc381.webp\" width=\"825\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:493px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>“What happened here?”</h3>\n\n<p>To expand on the question we had one paragraph ago, we should look into the fundamental difference between fiction and reality. That difference is, fiction has to make sense.</p>\n\n<p>For your fictional world to make sense, you must have a history behind each element within it. In other words, as a level artist, your job is to infuse history into your game environments.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/227a5bb2c74e16b083af.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/227a5bb2c74e16b083af.webp\" width=\"1120\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> –</strong> <strong>What happened?</strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Doom</em> (2016) takes place on the desolate landscapes of Mars, overrun by demons. Before the player character woke up to fight back the legions of Hell, many Mars citizens had fallen victim to them. The top picture gives us a glimpse in the final moments of two people hopelessly praying for the nightmare to pass.</p>\n\n<p>The example from <em>Bioshock 2</em> is easier to work out, so I’ll leave it to you. J</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fe728ab43f1bf333e42e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fe728ab43f1bf333e42e.webp\" width=\"879\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:497px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>When to use it?</h3>\n\n<p>Generally, environmental storytelling is most effective when the player has enough mental bandwidth to process it. Therefore, it is recommended in low-stress areas, such as an explorable room or a puzzle room.</p>\n\n<p>Combat arenas are stressful, but they still can have stories. Sometimes, the combat isn’t immediately triggered upon the player’s arrival, but instead waits until certain conditions are met. Other times, players are allowed to stay and explore the area after defeating all the enemies. In cases like these, we can add story elements to our combat arenas.</p>\n\n<p>In short, you need to research your level brief carefully and talk to your designer beforehand.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ee7881a8333cc4dfb7f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ee7881a8333cc4dfb7f.webp\" width=\"875\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:524px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>What to use it for?</h3>\n\n<p>There are many uses for environmental storytelling, but here we will focus on 3 common goals:</p>\n\n<p>1.<strong>Teach the player a gameplay mechanic</strong></p>\n\n<p>2.<strong>Warn the player of imminent danger</strong></p>\n\n<p>3.<strong>Develop the game world</strong></p>\n\n<p>Keep in mind that it’s possible achieve these goals at the same time.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/db5a8798b3bedfa56597.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/db5a8798b3bedfa56597.webp\" width=\"496\" height=\"310\" style=\"--source-width:496px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a>So, that's how we defeat these monsters.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b4f25e56e55e0fb2cd77.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b4f25e56e55e0fb2cd77.webp\" width=\"496\" height=\"279\" style=\"--source-width:496px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a>A love that once was</p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Teach the player a gameplay mechanic</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>The dead body on the electric fence tells us to avoid it. And the sawblade cutting the zombie in half tells us how to use it.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c71a4bf7b99c2d526281.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c71a4bf7b99c2d526281.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"369\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The Undead Settlement in <em>Dark Souls 3 </em>has a path littered with giant arrows. Once players get near this area, those arrows will rain from the sky. Turns out, there is a giant sniping them from afar. This detail not only enriched the world but also had a gameplay function.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/929fb00ebc2fb96e5c3c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/929fb00ebc2fb96e5c3c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Warn the player of imminent danger</strong></p>\n\n<p>The Museum level in <em>The Last of Us 2</em> starts as a fun day off for the main characters, but toward the end of the level, Ellie slowly uncovers the morbid fate of those who once lived there. What happens afterward? Let’s not spoil it.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/eac637e6ae40bbb06dd0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/eac637e6ae40bbb06dd0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Below is the lair of the Broodmother boss in <em>Grounded</em>. As you go deeper into her dwelling, you will see other insects—the ones that gave you trouble before—trapped in her interlaced web network. The scenery is a warning statement for what’s to come once you awake the sleeping beast inside.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8ec844095295a2f79b75.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8ec844095295a2f79b75.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Develop the game world</strong></p>\n\n<p>The events in <em>Returnal</em> transpire on multiple alien locations. The weathered remains on these places give us a glimpse into an ancient civilization that once was.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d7eb01d8bdffebaf2250.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d7eb01d8bdffebaf2250.webp\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p>Here is another example from <em>Dark Souls 3</em>.</p>\n\n<p>The staircase leading up to the castle here is divided into 3 lanes, with the middle lane having larger steps than the others. The story behind this is humans and giants used to coexist in this are, so they built this staircase with different sizes for different species.</p>\n\n<p>The different-sized steps have no gameplay impact whatsoever, but they add to the immersion of the game.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ddd0a3be6fc637b57625.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ddd0a3be6fc637b57625.webp\" width=\"877\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:497px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The 14th chapter in <em>Uncharted 4</em> has Nathan Drake visit a lost city once occupied by pirates.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d8a0f79c020e32dee192.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d8a0f79c020e32dee192.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>In <em>Breath of The Wild</em>, there is an abandoned house built next to a horseback archery course. Entering this house, the player will find a chest containing some arrows. Compounded with the fact that it’s built on a main road, we can assume that this place used to be a business for training archers.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/030ca13b18a3660dac29.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/030ca13b18a3660dac29.webp\" width=\"388\" height=\"218\" style=\"--source-width:153px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3162c2cf9c8a01de14a9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3162c2cf9c8a01de14a9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Sometimes, it can just be some soldier playing a harmonica during his short-lived respite, admist a warzone. From <em>The Division 2</em>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4306c0a3435f6d50f87d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4306c0a3435f6d50f87d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"277\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Toolbox overview</h3>\n\n<p>In this section, we will focus on a handful of basic tools to keep things manageable.</p>\n\n<p>1.<strong>Set dressing objects</strong></p>\n\n<p>2.<strong>Character models</strong></p>\n\n<p>3.<strong>Text</strong><strong>s</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7b70cc1877eb6d2e8a14.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7b70cc1877eb6d2e8a14.webp\" width=\"967\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<h4>Set dressing objects</h4>\n\n<p>Props are the most common tool for environmental storytelling. Though using inanimate objects, the story we tell is likely a human one.</p>\n\n<ul><li>“Who lived there?”</li><li>“Who were they? What were they like?”</li><li>“What did they do for a living?”</li><li>“Did they have any hobbies?”</li><li>“How long has this place been abandoned?”</li><li>“Why was it abandoned?”</li><li>Etc.</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b48dc048384167ad966f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b48dc048384167ad966f.webp\" width=\"912\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:546px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Set dressing objects</strong></p>\n\n<p>In <em>Borderlands 3</em>, you can sometimes stumble across makeshift resting places of your enemies.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/55cf82957e54375c5351.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/55cf82957e54375c5351.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The torture devices and medicines tell us what this prison is meant for. From <em>Resident Evil Village</em>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4fce1f0fddece4698efc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4fce1f0fddece4698efc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>In the left picture, we see some makeshift tables, beers, and chairs, which tell us that a group of people used to spend their respite drinking here. In the right picture, we see coffee and a remote control on the couch, hinting that whoever lives here doesn’t leave the living room often; and in the family picture behind, the husband’s face is covered. From <em>XCOM 2</em>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/75a472cdcbbccd9aa2a6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/75a472cdcbbccd9aa2a6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"302\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>This bedroom tells you a lot about its owner. They are fond of horror movies, they are a big fan of music, and they probably like reading.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/70fcd4a73222f6a8a581.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/70fcd4a73222f6a8a581.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"297\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>In contrast, whoever lives there doesn’t seem to enjoy reading.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f553c268ccd0acde2bd8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f553c268ccd0acde2bd8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"560\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 28\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>You probably know what these are for.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dff1adcefb978d10ebb6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dff1adcefb978d10ebb6.webp\" width=\"684\" height=\"386\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 29\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>This group of people, on the other hand, prefer ducks. And they also have a sense of humor.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f5a0fe8f3c2e6f74555e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f5a0fe8f3c2e6f74555e.webp\" width=\"912\" height=\"386\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 30\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/308588012b5775cba403.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/308588012b5775cba403.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"435\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 31\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>Character models</h4>\n\n<p>Although inanimate character models are technically set dressing objects, they’re often used to depict unpleasant circumstances. Adding a dead body to a scene automatically makes it more intense and alerts the player.</p>\n\n<p>Working in this category means the level artist has to find a reason why and how the body ended up in a certain place.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b987aa701128140aec16.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b987aa701128140aec16.webp\" width=\"833\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:498px\" alt=\"Illustration 32\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><em><strong> –</strong></em><strong> </strong><strong>Character models</strong></p>\n\n<p><em>Fallout</em> has plenty of skeletons. Like, a lot of them.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/60266de40e3c28cdedb5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/60266de40e3c28cdedb5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"333\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 33\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Dead bodies are sometimes used as “item boxes.”</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/040bb38e66b47457f8e0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/040bb38e66b47457f8e0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 34\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Can you tell how these people died? From <em>Bioshock</em>.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d787249770c4f0a1ff52.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d787249770c4f0a1ff52.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 35\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/51db6b3aded822b07806.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/51db6b3aded822b07806.webp\" width=\"792\" height=\"470\" style=\"--source-width:368px\" alt=\"Illustration 36\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><em>Diablo II </em>and <em>Diablo II: Resurrected</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/926d778a2502230c8672.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/926d778a2502230c8672.webp\" width=\"634\" height=\"358\" style=\"--source-width:193px\" alt=\"Illustration 37\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b7dc1d548a6750765491.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b7dc1d548a6750765491.webp\" width=\"834\" height=\"470\" style=\"--source-width:368px\" alt=\"Illustration 38\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d030e3fc200003e0c31d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d030e3fc200003e0c31d.webp\" width=\"694\" height=\"353\" style=\"--source-width:193px\" alt=\"Illustration 39\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/187bb0cf875f7ba0d44b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/187bb0cf875f7ba0d44b.webp\" width=\"836\" height=\"470\" style=\"--source-width:368px\" alt=\"Illustration 40\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a1c29de09a48612ec14a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a1c29de09a48612ec14a.webp\" width=\"834\" height=\"470\" style=\"--source-width:193px\" alt=\"Illustration 41\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b3d5f852a6c6c85d07c1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b3d5f852a6c6c85d07c1.webp\" width=\"626\" height=\"353\" style=\"--source-width:368px\" alt=\"Illustration 42\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/238c69cc6efb8d144a1d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/238c69cc6efb8d144a1d.webp\" width=\"848\" height=\"478\" style=\"--source-width:193px\" alt=\"Illustration 43\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h4>Text</h4>\n\n<p>Although the golden rule of storytelling is “Show, don’t tell,” we don’t have to avoid using words entirely. Words are ever-present in our daily life. From our phones, our computer screens, to banners and signage, and even graffiti, the real world is filled to the brim with texts. In a way, texts make a world believable because they imply the presence of human (or a sentient being from elsewhere).</p>\n\n<h4>“Yeah, welcome, Leon. Bet you had a kickass first day.”</h4>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e0e2126c4cce3828548e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e0e2126c4cce3828548e.webp\" width=\"523\" height=\"294\" style=\"--source-width:523px\" alt=\"Illustration 44\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE – </strong><strong>Text</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/69e4673eded2a31e90a1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/69e4673eded2a31e90a1.webp\" width=\"834\" height=\"470\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 45\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/82aaab22315eca0db8c2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/82aaab22315eca0db8c2.webp\" width=\"602\" height=\"340\" style=\"--source-width:334px\" alt=\"Illustration 46\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><br>“Stop feeding us LIES! GIVE US OUR RATIONS!”</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ecff92a8d076b7db5b5c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ecff92a8d076b7db5b5c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"649\" style=\"--source-width:227px\" alt=\"Illustration 47\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><br>“DEATH for FREEDOM”</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3705ae41633e0c4df51e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3705ae41633e0c4df51e.webp\" width=\"396\" height=\"223\" style=\"--source-width:334px\" alt=\"Illustration 48\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a>“Another 3 soldiers dead-what’s it going to take? GIVE UP!”</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/19991e7a01c76b461b6c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/19991e7a01c76b461b6c.webp\" width=\"396\" height=\"224\" style=\"--source-width:227px\" alt=\"Illustration 49\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>“F***ING DIE PIG”</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><em>The Last of Us 2</em>, as it takes place in the same world, continues to inject story into its environments using written language.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/10d3a9ebfed5f3600d02.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/10d3a9ebfed5f3600d02.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 50\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>Left 4 Dead</em> also depicts the aftermath of a zombie outbreak. There is an area in the game where the player can find plenty of notes left behind by other survivors.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/86a7a91536b1adb61c43.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/86a7a91536b1adb61c43.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 51\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>As with the <em>Bioshock 2</em> example earlier, video games seem to have a special place for father-daughter relationship.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/efe703634d5d8675812c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/efe703634d5d8675812c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 52\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>In this sequence from <em>Resident Evil Village</em>, the antagonist continuously teases the players with provocative signposts.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/150bdbd906f5459797d1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/150bdbd906f5459797d1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 53\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>“This way papa!”                                                                                                                                        “The show must go on!”</p>\n\n<p>Continuing with <em>Resident Evil Village</em>, here is an optional puzzle in one of the houses in the titular village.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>You find a lock in an abandoned house.</p></td><td><p>Look around and you find a picture that says:</p></td><td><p>And you see the code: 070408</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e45418a48ca89d6f0e42.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e45418a48ca89d6f0e42.webp\" width=\"410\" height=\"232\" style=\"--source-width:187px\" alt=\"Illustration 54\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c30cb08a7ec21c2762c8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c30cb08a7ec21c2762c8.webp\" width=\"410\" height=\"232\" style=\"--source-width:198px\" alt=\"Illustration 55\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><br>“Look out the window.”</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/44d6d7325be7809e9ced.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/44d6d7325be7809e9ced.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"951\" style=\"--source-width:170px\" alt=\"Illustration 56\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Advertis</strong><strong>e</strong><strong>ments</strong></p>\n\n<p>Advertisements can be considered the cousin of texts in storytelling. The typeface, the language, the lighting scheme reveal to us how the locals in an area live, what they are interested in, how they approach their interests, etc.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/932bb143aac941393e17.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/932bb143aac941393e17.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 57\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
